{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"body":"## Root cause: the MCP container is OOM-killed by concurrent store-wide reads\n\n**Server-side evidence (AC1), 2026-10-28.** Reproduced from a Claude Code session: 9 parallel `next_batch` calls at about 17:46 PDT all failed `MCP server \"rebar\" is not connected`, and every later call failed too. On the box (read-only SSM):\n\n- `kernel: Memory cgroup out of memory: Killed process 1648385 (rebar-mcp) ... anon-rss:3733764kB` at 17:47:26 PDT. Container memory limit is 3,892,314,112 bytes (3.63 GiB). Docker restarted it (`restarts=1`); nginx served 502 until about 17:48.\n- Autodeploy ran only no-ops in that window (`up to date (f8ddcaf88e6...); no-op`), so a deploy did not cause the restart.\n- `rebar-health` RSS samples: about 0.2 to 1.9 GB through the afternoon, then from 15:34 PDT a plateau of 3.16 to 3.41 GB that never came back down, until the kill. A fresh process is about 0.4 GB.\n- The same OOM kill appears on 2026-10-23 13:23 and 2026-10-24 06:31 (kernel log, times as logged).\n- The client then keeps the server marked disconnected for the session, so single calls also fail after the batch. The 2026-10-18 report of single calls working right after may reflect a faster restart or reconnect; not checked.\n\n**Mechanism, measured locally** (one process, isolated clone of the tickets store, 5,759 tickets, worktree at `f8ddcaf88e6`). MCP runs every sync tool on an AnyIO worker thread (`_mcp_health.offload_sync_tools`; AnyIO's default limit is 40 threads), so a batch runs truly concurrently.\n\n| Scenario | Peak RSS | RSS after | Wall |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 `next_batch` | 631 MB | 465 MB | 45 s |\n| 9 `next_batch`, sequential | 773 MB | 552 MB | 109 s |\n| 9 `next_batch`, concurrent | 4,506 MB | 3,069 MB | 106 s |\n| 9 `next_batch`, at most 2 at once | 1,275 MB | 747 MB | 71 s |\n| 9 `next_batch`, at most 3 at once | 1,750 MB | 1,220 MB | 84 s |\n| 9 `list_tickets(status=open)`, concurrent | 2,955 MB | 1,716 MB | 40 s |\n| 1 `reduce_all_tickets` alone | 399 MB | 273 MB | 3.7 s |\n\nEach `next_batch` reduces the entire store (`reduce_all_tickets(exclude_archived=False, exclude_deleted=False)`) to find one epic's descendants, then calls `list_states` again. Concurrent calls each hold their own full copy, so peak memory scales with concurrency. This is not specific to `next_batch`: 9 concurrent `list_tickets` come close to the limit too. Memory also stays high after the calls end (3.07 GB retained after the concurrent run), which matches the box's 2-hour plateau, so the next batch starts near the limit.\n\nWhat it does not show: what produced the 15:34 PDT jump on the box. The container log does not tie requests to that time."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793235799007670001,"uuid":"81268df3-6904-460c-9588-78f60d09f347"}